{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"c0a42a4b1e2aa910","name":"card_event_harass","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_harass","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"HARASS","text":"Send a unit to hand. Convert it into a random unit of same cost if you have an air unit.","flavorText":"Between March 1942 and June 1943, the Luftwaffe launched persistent low-level bombing raids against Britain’s southern coast. Known to the British as “tip and run” attacks, these missions were flown by fast single-seat fighter-bombers, initially Bf 109s and later Fw 190s, from Jagdgeschwader 2 and Jagdgeschwader 26. These aircraft struck coastal towns, radar sites, airfields and shipping with surprise and speed, often avoiding detection until bombs had already been dropped. Despite never having more than 28 aircraft at a time, these units inflicted considerable disruption. British radar struggled to detect low-flying raiders and early warning systems proved inadequate. Anti-aircraft defenses expanded rapidly in response, from just 43 light guns in March 1942 to over 500 by the end of the year. Major attacks hit strategic targets like the Telecommunications Research Establishment at Worth Matravers and town centers from Torquay to Eastbourne. A single raid on Canterbury in October 1942 involved over 60 Fw 190s. While RAF Typhoons eventually claimed some successes, most losses occurred after the bombs had already been dropped. The campaign ended abruptly in June 1943 as German aircraft were redeployed to face the Allied threat in the Mediterranean. These attacks forced Britain to commit large resources for minimal German effort.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_harass","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_harass_title","SourceString":"HARASS","LocalizedString":"HARASS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_harass_text","SourceString":"Send a unit to hand. Convert it into a random unit of same cost if you have an air unit.","LocalizedString":"Send a unit to hand. Convert it into a random unit of same cost if you have an air unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_harass_history","SourceString":"Between March 1942 and June 1943, the Luftwaffe launched persistent low-level bombing raids against Britain’s southern coast. Known to the British as “tip and run” attacks, these missions were flown by fast single-seat fighter-bombers, initially Bf 109s and later Fw 190s, from Jagdgeschwader 2 and Jagdgeschwader 26. These aircraft struck coastal towns, radar sites, airfields and shipping with surprise and speed, often avoiding detection until bombs had already been dropped. Despite never having more than 28 aircraft at a time, these units inflicted considerable disruption. British radar struggled to detect low-flying raiders and early warning systems proved inadequate. Anti-aircraft defenses expanded rapidly in response, from just 43 light guns in March 1942 to over 500 by the end of the year. Major attacks hit strategic targets like the Telecommunications Research Establishment at Worth Matravers and town centers from Torquay to Eastbourne. A single raid on Canterbury in October 1942 involved over 60 Fw 190s. While RAF Typhoons eventually claimed some successes, most losses occurred after the bombs had already been dropped. The campaign ended abruptly in June 1943 as German aircraft were redeployed to face the Allied threat in the Mediterranean. These attacks forced Britain to commit large resources for minimal German effort.","LocalizedString":"Between March 1942 and June 1943, the Luftwaffe launched persistent low-level bombing raids against Britain’s southern coast. Known to the British as “tip and run” attacks, these missions were flown by fast single-seat fighter-bombers, initially Bf 109s and later Fw 190s, from Jagdgeschwader 2 and Jagdgeschwader 26. These aircraft struck coastal towns, radar sites, airfields and shipping with surprise and speed, often avoiding detection until bombs had already been dropped. Despite never having more than 28 aircraft at a time, these units inflicted considerable disruption. British radar struggled to detect low-flying raiders and early warning systems proved inadequate. Anti-aircraft defenses expanded rapidly in response, from just 43 light guns in March 1942 to over 500 by the end of the year. Major attacks hit strategic targets like the Telecommunications Research Establishment at Worth Matravers and town centers from Torquay to Eastbourne. A single raid on Canterbury in October 1942 involved over 60 Fw 190s. While RAF Typhoons eventually claimed some successes, most losses occurred after the bombs had already been dropped. The campaign ended abruptly in June 1943 as German aircraft were redeployed to face the Allied threat in the Mediterranean. These attacks forced Britain to commit large resources for minimal German effort."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Convert"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Germany/World_at_War/T_harass.T_harass","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Rebellion","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/HARRASS.HARRASS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":355.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_harass_photo","SourceString":"A formation of Bf-109 fighters flying over England during the Battle of Britain in 1940.","LocalizedString":"A formation of Bf-109 fighters flying over En